    <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"DiscussionForumPosting","headline":"[select] Don't reuse slot=\"\" and ::part(); behavior=\"\" is also strange","articleBody":"slot=\"\" and ::part() are used for custom elements. They are a separate namespace for authors to use.\r\n\r\nNative HTML elements get customized via normal child elements and pseudo-elements:\r\n\r\n- `\u003csummary\u003e` for `\u003cdetails\u003e`, `\u003coption\u003e` and `\u003coptgroup\u003e` for `\u003cselect\u003e`, `\u003clegend\u003e` for `\u003cfieldset\u003e`, ...\r\n- `::marker` for `\u003cdetails\u003e`, `::backdrop` for `\u003cdialog\u003e`, `::placeholder` for `\u003cinput\u003e`, etc. (This is maybe related to #645.)\r\n\r\nSimilarly, your use of a semi-generic `behavior=\"\"` attribute, which only has meaning within a certain parent context but can be used on any arbitrary element, is not idiomatic to HTML either. Is there any way to replace that with an idiomatic child-element pattern?","author":{"url":"https://github.com/domenic","@type":"Person","name":"domenic"},"datePublished":"2023-03-31T05:53:52.000Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"https://schema.org/CommentAction","userInteractionCount":40},"url":"https://github.com/702/open-ui/issues/702"}</script> 
