iewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"ROCKET","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"EYES","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}}],"__isNode":"IssueComment"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABh2vbRWgAqjE1MDE5NzcwMzA="},{"node":{"__typename":"IssueComment","databaseId":1502285370,"viewerDidAuthor":false,"issue":{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"I_kwDOCySmds5iRWQp","number":702,"locked":false,"databaseId":1648714793},"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"mfreed7","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/29559695?u=996bfc0df5b5e0a7a210631bfd2760f23fdc8912\u0026v=4","profileUrl":"https://github.com/mfreed7","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI5NTU5Njk1","name":"Mason Freed"},"id":"IC_kwDOCySmds5Ziw46","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1502285370","createdAt":"2023-04-10T20:33:57Z","authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","viewerCanDelete":false,"viewerCanMinimize":false,"viewerCanUnminimize":false,"viewerCanReport":false,"viewerCanReportToMaintainer":false,"viewerCanBlockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanUnblockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanBlockPersonally":false,"viewerCanUnblockPersonally":false,"viewerCanPin":false,"viewerCanUnpin":false,"viaApp":null,"minimizedReason":null,"showSpammyBadge":false,"createdViaEmail":false,"authorToRepoOwnerSponsorship":null,"viewerCanReadUserContentEdits":true,"lastEditedAt":null,"lastUserContentEdit":null,"pinnedBy":null,"body":"\u003e slot=\"\" and ::part() are used for custom elements. They are a separate namespace for authors to use.\r\n\r\nCan you comment more on this? `slot` and `part()` aren’t just for custom elements, right? They’re for shadow DOM, which the `\u003cselectmenu\u003e` contains. Part of what I liked about this proposal was that it isn’t co-opting these attributes for a different purpose, it is directly using them. I.e. the slot attribute actually slots that element into the UA shadow root. And ::part() exposes the parts. This would be the first native element with a well defined shadow structure designed for direct part replacement like this. But I think the way this works is exactly the same “namespace” as custom elements that use shadow DOM.\r\n\r\nA nice thing (IMHO) about using slotting attributes is that a semantically appropriate element such as a `\u003cbutton\u003e` or `\u003cinput\u003e` can be used, rather than a new element that presumably always acts like a button? ","bodyHTML":"\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eslot=\"\" and ::part() are used for custom elements. They are a separate namespace for authors to use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eCan you comment more on this? \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003epart()\u003c/code\u003e aren’t just for custom elements, right? They’re for shadow DOM, which the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectmenu\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e contains. Part of what I liked about this proposal was that it isn’t co-opting these attributes for a different purpose, it is directly using them. I.e. the slot attribute actually slots that element into the UA shadow root. And ::part() exposes the parts. This would be the first native element with a well defined shadow structure designed for direct part replacement like this. But I think the way this works is exactly the same “namespace” as custom elements that use shadow DOM.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eA nice thing (IMHO) about using slotting attributes is that a semantically appropriate element such as a \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;button\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;input\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e can be used, rather than a new element that presumably always acts like a button?\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"ce568c56461db0b18cd93a8ae29e3d3d8765ce5f7208c09dfd692448946e5040","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkxODY5NTEyODY=","name":"open-ui","owner":{"__typename":"Organization","id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc5OTYwOTcx","login":"openui","url":"https://github.com/openui"},"isPrivate":false,"viewerCanCreateIssues":false,"slashCommandsEnabled":false,"nameWithOwner":"openui/open-ui","databaseId":186951286},"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":3,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"Westbrook","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExNTY2NTc="},{"__typename":"User","login":"EisenbergEffect","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzMTQ4NQ=="},{"__typename":"User","login":"morewry","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEwNDMyOTE="}]}},{"content":"THUMBS_DOWN","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"LAUGH","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HOORAY","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"CONFUSED","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HEART","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"ROCKET","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"EYES","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}}],"__isNode":"IssueComment"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABh2zgEwgAqjE1MDIyODUzNzA="},{"node":{"__typename":"IssueComment","databaseId":1502545773,"viewerDidAuthor":false,"issue":{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"I_kwDOCySmds5iRWQp","number":702,"locked":false,"databaseId":1648714793},"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/617481?v=4","profileUrl":"https://github.com/domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ==","name":"Domenic Denicola"},"id":"IC_kwDOCySmds5Zjwdt","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1502545773","createdAt":"2023-04-11T01:14:22Z","authorAssociation":"CONTRIBUTOR","viewerCanDelete":false,"viewerCanMinimize":false,"viewerCanUnminimize":false,"viewerCanReport":false,"viewerCanReportToMaintainer":false,"viewerCanBlockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanUnblockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanBlockPersonally":false,"viewerCanUnblockPersonally":false,"viewerCanPin":false,"viewerCanUnpin":false,"viaApp":null,"minimizedReason":null,"showSpammyBadge":false,"createdViaEmail":false,"authorToRepoOwnerSponsorship":null,"viewerCanReadUserContentEdits":true,"lastEditedAt":"2023-04-11T01:14:37Z","lastUserContentEdit":{"editor":{"__typename":"User","url":"https://github.com/domenic","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"UCE_lALOCySmds5Zjwdtzi_BRpY"},"pinnedBy":null,"body":"\u003e Can you comment more on this? `slot` and `part()` aren’t just for custom elements, right? They’re for shadow DOM\r\n\r\nSorry, I mispoke, you're right. They're for shadow DOM. But, specifically by shadow DOM, I mean they're for DOM trees created by authors with `attachShadow()` (or declarative shadow DOM).\r\n\r\n\u003e which the `\u003cselectmenu\u003e` contains.\r\n\r\n`\u003cselectmenu\u003e` does not (or at least should not) contain shadow DOM, in the sense of \"something accessed and created through the normal shadow DOM APIs\". I expect that if it's a native HTML element, it might have a sort of \"user agent shadow root\", like various implementations have chosen to use when implementing controls like `\u003cinput type=date\u003e`, `\u003cdetails\u003e`, or `\u003cselect\u003e`. But this is just an implementation detail, and should not interfere with the normal web developer APIs. There should be no `shadowRoot` property; `slot=\"\"` and `::part()` should not get their meaning changed by the presence of these elements; etc.\r\n\r\n\u003e This would be the first native element with a well defined shadow structure designed for direct part replacement like this.\r\n\r\n`\u003cdetails\u003e` is arguably such an element, as is `\u003cselect\u003e` itself. Maybe even `\u003cli\u003e`, with its `::marker` being the replace-able part. The point is, the choice of underlying technology to implement those \"replaceable slots\" is an implementation detail. Chromium might have chosen to use shadow DOM for `\u003cselectmenu\u003e`, but that implementation-specific technology choice must not leak into the specification and start interfering with web-developer-facing technology like `slot=\"\"` or `::part()`.\r\n\r\n\u003e A nice thing (IMHO) about using slotting attributes is that a semantically appropriate element such as a `\u003cbutton\u003e` or `\u003cinput\u003e` can be used, rather than a new element that presumably always acts like a button?\r\n\r\nAgain, I don't think existing examples bear this out. `\u003csummary\u003e` is not exactly like a button. `\u003cbutton\u003e`s can have arbitrary behavior, but `\u003csummary\u003e` always expands/collapses its `\u003cdetails\u003e` element. I think that's the same intention for `\u003cselectmenubutton\u003e` as explained in https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1499336091 .","bodyHTML":"\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eCan you comment more on this? \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003epart()\u003c/code\u003e aren’t just for custom elements, right? They’re for shadow DOM\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eSorry, I mispoke, you're right. They're for shadow DOM. But, specifically by shadow DOM, I mean they're for DOM trees created by authors with \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eattachShadow()\u003c/code\u003e (or declarative shadow DOM).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003ewhich the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectmenu\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e contains.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectmenu\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e does not (or at least should not) contain shadow DOM, in the sense of \"something accessed and created through the normal shadow DOM APIs\". I expect that if it's a native HTML element, it might have a sort of \"user agent shadow root\", like various implementations have chosen to use when implementing controls like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;input type=date\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;details\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;select\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e. But this is just an implementation detail, and should not interfere with the normal web developer APIs. There should be no \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eshadowRoot\u003c/code\u003e property; \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot=\"\"\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e should not get their meaning changed by the presence of these elements; etc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThis would be the first native element with a well defined shadow structure designed for direct part replacement like this.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;details\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e is arguably such an element, as is \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;select\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e itself. Maybe even \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;li\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e, with its \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::marker\u003c/code\u003e being the replace-able part. The point is, the choice of underlying technology to implement those \"replaceable slots\" is an implementation detail. Chromium might have chosen to use shadow DOM for \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectmenu\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e, but that implementation-specific technology choice must not leak into the specification and start interfering with web-developer-facing technology like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot=\"\"\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eA nice thing (IMHO) about using slotting attributes is that a semantically appropriate element such as a \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;button\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;input\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e can be used, rather than a new element that presumably always acts like a button?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eAgain, I don't think existing examples bear this out. \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;summary\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e is not exactly like a button. \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;button\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003es can have arbitrary behavior, but \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;summary\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e always expands/collapses its \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;details\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e element. I think that's the same intention for \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectmenubutton\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e as explained in \u003ca class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"1648714793\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/openui/open-ui/issues/702/hovercard?comment_id=1499336091\u0026amp;comment_type=issue_comment\" href=\"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1499336091\"\u003e#702 (comment)\u003c/a\u003e .\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"220f616480064355e5a1df90c028a4fb156048d0f7e3f7728a07646b3baad4ae","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id