rs":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HEART","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"ROCKET","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"EYES","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}}],"__isNode":"IssueComment"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABh3fXqTgAqjE1MDYxMDE5MzE="},{"node":{"__typename":"UnlabeledEvent","__isTimelineEvent":"UnlabeledEvent","databaseId":8990900992,"createdAt":"2023-04-12T23:40:38Z","actor":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2ODE0Njc=","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7681467?s=64\u0026u=975aa36c2bf890f0c3f43db7a9d653fd4ad5a5f2\u0026v=4","login":"josepharhar","profileResourcePath":"/josepharhar","__typename":"User","name":"Joey Arhar"},"label":{"id":"MDU6TGFiZWwyMDM4OTkyNDA4","nameHTML":"agenda+","name":"agenda+","color":"c5def5","description":"Use this label if you'd like the topic to be added to the meeting agenda"},"id":"UNLE_lADOCySmds5iRWQpzwAAAAIX5kMA","__isNode":"UnlabeledEvent"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABh3fXtPABqjg5OTA5MDA5OTI="},{"node":{"__typename":"CrossReferencedEvent","__isTimelineEvent":"CrossReferencedEvent","databaseId":1284870159,"createdAt":"2023-05-10T01:39:27Z","actor":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NTI0NA==","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/865244?s=64\u0026u=1cd703e477eca7a265eee5ce7d7999971de65c80\u0026v=4","login":"gregwhitworth","profileResourcePath":"/gregwhitworth","__typename":"User","name":"Greg Whitworth"},"source":{"__typename":"Issue","__isNode":"Issue","id":"I_kwDOCySmds5k64jZ"},"willCloseTarget":false,"referencedAt":"2023-05-10T01:39:26Z","innerSource":{"__typename":"Issue","id":"I_kwDOCySmds5k64jZ","titleHTML":"Inconsistent ordering of anatomy slot and parts","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/743","number":743,"stateReason":"COMPLETED","repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkxODY5NTEyODY=","name":"open-ui","isPrivate":false,"owner":{"__typename":"Organization","login":"openui","id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc5OTYwOTcx"}},"__isNode":"Issue"},"__isNode":"CrossReferencedEvent","id":"CRE_kwDOCySmds5MlZAP"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABiANQMJgCqjEyODQ4NzAxNTk="},{"node":{"__typename":"IssueComment","databaseId":1541581270,"viewerDidAuthor":false,"issue":{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"I_kwDOCySmds5iRWQp","number":702,"locked":false,"databaseId":1648714793},"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"lukewarlow","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/32498324?u=33625fe335fbb6a8a62271bcc211afc447904e8a\u0026v=4","profileUrl":"https://github.com/lukewarlow","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyNDk4MzI0","name":"Luke Warlow"},"id":"IC_kwDOCySmds5b4qnW","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1541581270","createdAt":"2023-05-10T08:35:36Z","authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","viewerCanDelete":false,"viewerCanMinimize":false,"viewerCanUnminimize":false,"viewerCanReport":false,"viewerCanReportToMaintainer":false,"viewerCanBlockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanUnblockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanBlockPersonally":false,"viewerCanUnblockPersonally":false,"viewerCanPin":false,"viewerCanUnpin":false,"viaApp":null,"minimizedReason":null,"showSpammyBadge":false,"createdViaEmail":false,"authorToRepoOwnerSponsorship":null,"viewerCanReadUserContentEdits":true,"lastEditedAt":null,"lastUserContentEdit":null,"pinnedBy":null,"body":"Just chiming in from a web developer perspective obviously I understand there's trade-offs with each API.\n\nI think I prefer treating it as a customizable shadow root rather than like the old style html elements. The main concern seems to be this isn't how HTML behaves currently but isn't that the problem that open-ui is trying to solve in that there isn't a proper specification for structure and stylability?\n\nTake the file input for example we can currently  customise the selector button using a (only fairly recently) standard pseudo element but we can't for example remove the label entirely or customise its text even. This would be better as a more defined shadow root like selectmenu.\n\nIf a proper structural spec appears for HTML elements then the worries about exposing implementation details (presumably for forwards compatibility reasons) mostly go away?\n\nAlso adding a new CSS pseudo element or actual html element for every single customisable part of each html element doesn't seem like a great design to me. Even adding the search html element required accessibility tools to update and various other software changes. Adding loads more elements each with their own contextual semantics seems like it might end up in a place where those kinds of tools are constantly playing catch-up?","bodyHTML":"\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eJust chiming in from a web developer perspective obviously I understand there's trade-offs with each API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI think I prefer treating it as a customizable shadow root rather than like the old style html elements. The main concern seems to be this isn't how HTML behaves currently but isn't that the problem that open-ui is trying to solve in that there isn't a proper specification for structure and stylability?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eTake the file input for example we can currently  customise the selector button using a (only fairly recently) standard pseudo element but we can't for example remove the label entirely or customise its text even. This would be better as a more defined shadow root like selectmenu.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eIf a proper structural spec appears for HTML elements then the worries about exposing implementation details (presumably for forwards compatibility reasons) mostly go away?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eAlso adding a new CSS pseudo element or actual html element for every single customisable part of each html element doesn't seem like a great design to me. Even adding the search html element required accessibility tools to update and various other software changes. Adding loads more elements each with their own contextual semantics seems like it might end up in a place where those kinds of tools are constantly playing catch-up?\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"7852c13dac5311e9113404172ded73f140216dcafb770225dd665a4d79f1db6f","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkxODY5NTEyODY=","name":"open-ui","owner":{"__typename":"Organization","id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc5OTYwOTcx","login":"openui","url":"https://github.com/openui"},"isPrivate":false,"viewerCanCreateIssues":false,"slashCommandsEnabled":false,"nameWithOwner":"openui/open-ui","databaseId":186951286},"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":2,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"morewry","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEwNDMyOTE="},{"__typename":"User","login":"fallaciousreasoning","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2NzgwMjQ="}]}},{"content":"THUMBS_DOWN","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":1,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"bleper","__isNode":"User","id":"U_kgDOB8EbLQ"}]}},{"content":"LAUGH","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HOORAY","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"CONFUSED","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HEART","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"ROCKET","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"EYES","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}}],"__isNode":"IssueComment"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABiATNL8AAqjE1NDE1ODEyNzA="},{"node":{"__typename":"IssueComment","databaseId":1541856771,"viewerDidAuthor":false,"issue":{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"I_kwDOCySmds5iRWQp","number":702,"locked":false,"databaseId":1648714793},"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"keithamus","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/118266?u=5a7fb2f3d1ecc7dcd73f43f4b478cb4938e6572c\u0026v=4","profileUrl":"https://github.com/keithamus","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExODI2Ng==","name":"Keith Cirkel"},"id":"IC_kwDOCySmds5b5t4D","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1541856771","createdAt":"2023-05-10T10:07:46Z","authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","viewerCanDelete":false,"viewerCanMinimize":false,"viewerCanUnminimize":false,"viewerCanReport":false,"viewerCanReportToMaintainer":false,"viewerCanBlockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanUnblockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanBlockPersonally":false,"viewerCanUnblockPersonally":false,"viewerCanPin":false,"viewerCanUnpin":false,"viaApp":null,"minimizedReason":null,"showSpammyBadge":false,"createdViaEmail":false,"authorToRepoOwnerSponsorship":null,"viewerCanReadUserContentEdits":true,"lastEditedAt":"2023-05-10T10:16:58Z","lastUserContentEdit":{"editor":{"__typename":"User","url":"https://github.com/keithamus","login":"keithamus","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExODI2Ng=="},"id":"UCE_lALOCySmds5b5t4DzjEaji8"},"pinnedBy":null,"body":"I'm not sure there's a appreciable practical difference in favour of using `::part()`/`slot=`. Domenic is talking about avoiding the `::part` and `slot` syntax, as it should be free to live within the realm of user-authored ShadowDOM, and the browser shouldn't introduce built ins using this as it is _yet another surface_ for potential webcompat issues. In other words `::part()`/`slot=` should be like `data-` attributes, and `-` in element names; an effective safe ground for userland, with a handshake agreement from specs not to collide.\r\n\r\n`::part()` is an ergonomic tradeoff between webcompat safety and CSS complexity at the cost of developer ergonomics. Those concerns aren't present when using \"bare\" pseudo selectors. Instead of `::part(foo)`, browsers can introduce syntax like `::foo` without webcompat issues, without the ergonomic tradeoff of `::part()` and without the same CSS complexity concerns (it will be a well defined extension to CSS rather than a custom userland one).\r\n\r\nLikewise `slot=` is a declarative mechanism for ShadowDOM authors to allow re-arranging of nodes to fit within a template structure. The browser doesn't _need_ this as it can construct these based on other serialisable steps, such as the tag name (which userland can do, but it requires JS).\r\n\r\nThe rest of the mechanics are identical; older elements (like `\u003cinput\u003e`) lacking well defined structural markup needn't set a precedent. Newer elements like `\u003cdetails\u003e`/`\u003csummary\u003e` can instead. \r\n\r\nThe concerns of adding more pseudo selectors vs using part seem rather unimportant; pedagogically speaking webdevs will have to learn either one; if I want to style a marker learning to target either `::part(marker)` or `::marker` is essentially the same mental space, except for those who haven't had exposure to authoring ShadowDOM would need to learn new concepts like `::part()`.\r\n\r\nThe other concern of using `::part()`/`slot` is that it makes it harder to differentiate, within a CSS file, which declarations drive userland and which drive the browser. A declaration of `::marker {}` has certain guarantees about the elements it will style, and the safety \u0026 cadence of which new elements will end up being targeted by that selector. The same is not true for `::part(marker) {}` which could conflict with arbitrary elements.","bodyHTML":"\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI'm not sure there's a appreciable practical difference in favour of using \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot=\u003c/code\u003e. Domenic is talking about avoiding the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot\u003c/code\u003e syntax, as it should be free to live within the realm of user-authored ShadowDOM, and the browser shouldn't introduce built ins using this as it is \u003cem\u003eyet another surface\u003c/em\u003e for potential webcompat issues. In other words \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot=\u003c/code\u003e should be like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003edata-\u003c/code\u003e attributes, and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e-\u003c/code\u003e in element names; an effective safe ground for userland, with a handshake agreement from specs not to collide.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e is an ergonomic tradeoff between webcompat safety and CSS complexity at the cost of developer ergonomics. Those concerns aren't present when using \"bare\" pseudo selectors. Instead of \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part(foo)\u003c/code\u003e, browsers can introduce syntax like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::foo\u003c/code\u003e without webcompat issues, without the ergonomic tradeoff of \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e and without the same CSS complexity concerns (it will be a well defined extension to CSS rather than a custom userland one).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eLikewise \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot=\u003c/code\u003e is a declarative mechanism for ShadowDOM authors to allow re-arranging of nodes to fit within a template structure. The browser doesn't \u003cem\u003eneed\u003c/em\u003e this as it can construct these based on other serialisable steps, such as the tag name (which userland can do, but it requires JS).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThe rest of the mechanics are identical; older elements (like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;input\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e) lacking well defined structural markup needn't set a precedent. Newer elements like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;details\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;summary\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e can instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThe concerns of adding more pseudo selectors vs using part seem rather unimportant; pedagogically speaking webdevs will have to learn either one; if I want to style a marker learning to target either \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part(marker)\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::marker\u003c/code\u003e is essentially the same mental space, except for those who haven't had exposure to authoring ShadowDOM would need to learn new concepts like \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThe other concern of using \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eslot\u003c/code\u003e is that it makes it harder to differentiate, within a CSS file, which declarations drive userland and which drive the browser. A declaration of \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::marker {}\u003c/code\u003e has certain guarantees about the elements it will style, and the safety \u0026amp; cadence of which new elements will end up being targeted by that selector. The same is not true for \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e::part(marker) {}\u003c/code\u003e which could conflict with arbitrary elements.\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"13c6d3b6ff138c2a2f3f79c08d97f3f911a4b7648a3a8cf7d62e7b1a3a02b8da","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkxODY5NTEyODY=","name":"open-ui","owner":{"__typename":"Organization","id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc5OTYwOTcx","login":"openui","url":"https://github.com/openui"},"isPrivate":false,"viewerCanCreateIssues":false,"slashCommandsEnabled":false,"nameWithOwner":"openui/open-ui","databaseId":186951286},"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":5,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},{"__typename":"User","login":"scottaohara","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQxNTI1MTQ="},{"__typename":"User","login":"bathos","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYyNTczNTY="},{"__typename":"User","login":"garretmh","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1MjYwMTM="},{"__typename":"User","login":"bleper","__isNode":"User","id":"U_kgDOB8EbLQ"}]}},{"content":"THUMBS_DOWN","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"LAUGH","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"HOORAY","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"CONFUSED","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":1,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"morewry","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEwNDMyOTE="}]}},{"content":"HEART","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":3,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"bathos","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYyNTczNTY="},{"__typename":"User","login":"gregwhitworth","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NTI0NA=="},{"__typename":"User","login":"bleper","__isNode":"User","id":"U_kgDOB8EbLQ"}]}},{"content":"ROCKET","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}},{"content":"EYES","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":0,"nodes":[]}}],"__isNode":"IssueComment"},"cursor":"Y3Vyc29yOnYyOpPPAAABiAUhkVAAqjE1NDE4NTY3NzE="},{"node":{"__typename":"IssueComment","databaseId":1635075799,"viewerDidAuthor":false,"issue":{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"domenic","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzQ4MQ=="},"id":"I_kwDOCySmds5iRWQp","number":702,"locked":false,"databaseId":1648714793},"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"josepharhar","avatarUrl":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7681467?u=975aa36c2bf890f0c3f43db7a9d653fd4ad5a5f2\u0026v=4","profileUrl":"https://github.com/josepharhar","id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2ODE0Njc=","name":"Joey Arhar"},"id":"IC_kwDOCySmds5hdUbX","url":"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/702#issuecomment-1635075799","createdAt":"2023-07-14T00:08:33Z","authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","viewerCanDelete":false,"viewerCanMinimize":false,"viewerCanUnminimize":false,"viewerCanReport":false,"viewerCanReportToMaintainer":false,"viewerCanBlockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanUnblockFromOrg":false,"viewerCanBlockPersonally":false,"viewerCanUnblockPersonally":false,"viewerCanPin":false,"viewerCanUnpin":false,"viaApp":null,"minimizedReason":null,"showSpammyBadge":false,"createdViaEmail":false,"authorToRepoOwnerSponsorship":null,"viewerCanReadUserContentEdits":true,"lastEditedAt":null,"lastUserContentEdit":null,"pinnedBy":null,"body":"A few of us have been working on a doc which walks through some ideas for new elements, including selectmenu, to see how using slots/behaviors vs creating new elements works for each of them: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1b1wTaoNwUY4sFJQZE0V1nLA7pve16WnYRipbBdzXpVU/edit\r\n\r\nUsing ::part and slots is helpful because:\r\n- It is very clean for new elements which have a lot of sub-parts \r\n- They're existing standardized and implemented technologies\r\n- Enables web developers to learn web components while using native HTML elements\r\n\r\nI see that ::part and slot collide with user-authored stuff, so what if we used a different names?\r\nWe actually already resolved to use \"::behavior\" instead of ::part in this issue: https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/645\r\nAs for slot, we could have another name like \"part\" or \"component\" or \"module\".\r\n\r\nHaving a separate behavior attribute also enables certain use cases - see the \"split button\" use case in the linked doc.\r\n\r\n\u003e your use of a semi-generic behavior=\"\" attribute, which only has meaning within a certain parent context but can be used on any arbitrary element, is not idiomatic to HTML either. Is there any way to replace that with an idiomatic child-element pattern?\r\n\r\nWe could sort of invert the structure in order to avoid having attributes which only have meaning within a certain parent context like this:\r\n\r\nWith behavior attribute:\r\n```html\r\n\u003cselectmenu\u003e\r\n  \u003cbutton slot=button\u003e\r\n    \u003cspan behavior=selected-value\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\r\n  \u003c/button\u003e\r\n\u003c/selectmenu\u003e\r\n```\r\nWith specialized attribute to invert stucture:\r\n```html\r\n\u003cselectmenu selected-value=myselectedvalue\u003e\r\n  \u003cbutton slot=button\u003e\r\n    \u003cspan id=myselectedvalue\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\r\n  \u003c/button\u003e\r\n\u003c/selectmenu\u003e\r\n```\r\nI don't know if this makes it any more idiomatic, but it certainly doesn't seem very ergonomic to have to add an element ID and wire it up in two places instead of just having `behavior=selected-value`.\r\n\r\nWe are planning on using the next OpenUI meeting to discuss the use cases and recommend yall read the doc","bodyHTML":"\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eA few of us have been working on a doc which walks through some ideas for new elements, including selectmenu, to see how using slots/behaviors vs creating new elements works for each of them: \u003ca href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1b1wTaoNwUY4sFJQZE0V1nLA7pve16WnYRipbBdzXpVU/edit\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1b1wTaoNwUY4sFJQZE0V1nLA7pve16WnYRipbBdzXpVU/edit\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eUsing ::part and slots is helpful because:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul dir=\"auto\"\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eIt is very clean for new elements which have a lot of sub-parts\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThey're existing standardized and implemented technologies\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eEnables web developers to learn web components while using native HTML elements\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI see that ::part and slot collide with user-authored stuff, so what if we used a different names?\u003cbr\u003e\nWe actually already resolved to use \"::behavior\" instead of ::part in this issue: \u003ca class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"1483203325\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/645\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/openui/open-ui/issues/645/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/645\"\u003e#645\u003c/a\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nAs for slot, we could have another name like \"part\" or \"component\" or \"module\".\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eHaving a separate behavior attribute also enables certain use cases - see the \"split button\" use case in the linked doc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eyour use of a semi-generic behavior=\"\" attribute, which only has meaning within a certain parent context but can be used on any arbitrary element, is not idiomatic to HTML either. Is there any way to replace that with an idiomatic child-element pattern?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eWe could sort of invert the structure in order to avoid having attributes which only have meaning within a certain parent context like this:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eWith behavior attribute:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"highlight highlight-text-html-basic notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"\u0026lt;selectmenu\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button slot=button\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span behavior=selected-value\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectmenu\u0026gt;\"\u003e\u003cpre class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003eselectmenu\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n  \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"pl-c1\"\u003eslot\u003c/span\u003e=\u003cspan class=\"pl-s\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n    \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003espan\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"pl-c1\"\u003ebehavior\u003c/span\u003e=\u003cspan class=\"pl-s\"\u003eselected-value\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003espan\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n  \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003eselectmenu\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eWith specialized attribute to invert stucture:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"highlight highlight-text-html-basic notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"\u0026lt;selectmenu selected-value=myselectedvalue\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button slot=button\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span id=myselectedvalue\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectmenu\u0026gt;\"\u003e\u003cpre class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003eselectmenu\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"pl-c1\"\u003eselected-value\u003c/span\u003e=\u003cspan class=\"pl-s\"\u003emyselectedvalue\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n  \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"pl-c1\"\u003eslot\u003c/span\u003e=\u003cspan class=\"pl-s\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n    \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003espan\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan class=\"pl-c1\"\u003eid\u003c/span\u003e=\u003cspan class=\"pl-s\"\u003emyselectedvalue\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003espan\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n  \u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003ebutton\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026lt;/\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-ent\"\u003eselectmenu\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"pl-kos\"\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI don't know if this makes it any more idiomatic, but it certainly doesn't seem very ergonomic to have to add an element ID and wire it up in two places instead of just having \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003ebehavior=selected-value\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eWe are planning on using the next OpenUI meeting to discuss the use cases and recommend yall read the doc\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"064afa1b5074cd202e794611e8edf9d129845ebcf0b6c6eb1cbaf6b05c865a98","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkxODY5NTEyODY=","name":"open-ui","owner":{"__typename":"Organization","id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc5OTYwOTcx","login":"openui","url":"https://github.com/openui"},"isPrivate":false,"viewerCanCreateIssues":false,"slashCommandsEnabled":false,"nameWithOwner":"openui/open-ui","databaseId":186951286},"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","viewerHasReacted":false,"reactors":{"totalCount":1,"nodes":[{"__typename":"User","login":"Totati","__isNode":"User","id":"MDQ6VX